A common myth says `.pipe()` ignores backpressure. It handles backpressure fine. The real difference is error handling and cleanup. With `.pipe()`, if one stream errors, nothing destroys the others, so they leak file descriptors and memory. You have to add `'error'` handlers and `destroy()` calls to every stream yourself.

`stream.pipeline()` (Node 10+) fixes this. It forwards data with backpressure, destroys every stream when one fails, and reports the first error.

```js
const { pipeline } = require("node:stream");
pipeline(src, transform, dest, (err) => {
  if (err) console.error("failed, all streams destroyed:", err.message);
});
```

We checked the difference: after the destination errors, `src.destroyed` is `false` with `.pipe()` and `true` with `pipeline()`. Use `pipeline` (or the promise version in `stream/promises`) for anything bigger than a toy script.

Each base class has internal methods you override. You never call them yourself; the stream machinery does.

- `Readable`: implement `_read(size)`. Call `this.push(chunk)` to supply data and `this.push(null)` to end.
- `Writable`: implement `_write(chunk, encoding, callback)`. Call `callback()` when you've handled the chunk, or `callback(err)` if it failed.
- `Transform`: implement `_transform(chunk, encoding, callback)`. Produce output with `this.push()` or `callback(null, output)`. Add `_flush(callback)` if you need to emit trailing data.

A `Transform` is already a `Duplex`, so it implements `_read` and `_write` for you. You only write `_transform`, plus `_flush` if you need it. The constructor shorthand uses the same names without the underscore:

```js
const { Transform } = require("node:stream");
const upper = new Transform({
  transform(chunk, enc, cb) { cb(null, chunk.toString().toUpperCase()); }
});
// write "ab", "cd" → reads "ABCD"
```

Some transforms build up state and need to emit a tail once the input ends. Node calls `_flush(callback)` after the last chunk has been written and before the `'end'` event, so you get one last chance to `this.push()` leftover output. A gzip Transform, for example, holds compression state and must flush its final bytes, or the archive is incomplete.

```js
const { Transform } = require("node:stream");
const buf = [];
const join = new Transform({
  transform(chunk, enc, cb) { buf.push(chunk.toString()); cb(); },
  flush(cb) { cb(null, buf.join(",")); } // emit the accumulated tail before 'end'
});
// write "a", "b", "c", then end() → reads "a,b,c"
```

`_flush` doesn't free memory, touch file descriptors, or reset the stream. Node streams can't be reused anyway.
